 #!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
MONSTER TAMER - touchscreen edition (Pygame)

MODES (chosen from a start screen):
    CLASSIC  - pick EASY / NORMAL / HARD. Each is a fixed round count
               with its own monster difficulty; survive it to win.
                   Easy   - 30 rounds, gentler monsters
                   Normal - 50 rounds, standard monsters
                   Hard   - 80 rounds, tougher monsters
    QUEST    - catch a specific checklist of monsters per stage; each
               of the 6 stages gets harder than the last, ending in a
               final "all quests complete" win.
    HARDCORE - Hard difficulty's monster stats, but Endless: no round
               cap at all. Play until you die.

SAVING: every mode has a SAVE button in the top bar during play. The
start screen shows a CONTINUE option whenever a save exists, which
resumes exactly where you left off (same cards, roster, XP, round).
A run's save is cleared automatically once that run ends (win or
lose), so CONTINUE never resumes a finished run.

Core turn loop (all modes): 4 cards are dealt. Tap through ALL 4, one
at a time, in any order - each one dims with a checkmark once you've
dealt with it. Once all 4 are handled, the next hand deals
automatically. No image assets required: monsters are drawn as a
skull icon, potions as a triangle-shaped bottle, and capture balls as
a simple two-tone orb.

Monster popups show whichever of these apply:
    CATCH  - if your Capture Level beats theirs (or it's round 1)
    BATTLE - if you have an active monster
    FLEE   - always available, zero risk. If a monster is too tough
             to catch or battle right now, FLEE is your only option -
             it never forces a loss.

You only lose if: your last monster dies in battle, or you go 2 full
rounds in a row with no active monster.

The window is resizable (F11 toggles fullscreen) so fonts, icons, and
buttons all scale up to fill your touchscreen.
"""

import json
import math
import os
import random
import sys

# Touchscreens can make SDL emit BOTH a real touch event and a
# synthesized mouse event for the same physical tap. Without this,
# a single tap could fire our tap-handling twice against the same
# (now-stale) button list, which used to crash the app.
os.environ.setdefault("SDL_TOUCH_MOUSE_EVENTS", "0")
os.environ.setdefault("SDL_MOUSE_TOUCH_EVENTS", "0")

